# Prefer `beforeEach` setup for test dependencies

When writing or updating tests, initialise dependency fakes once in `beforeEach` and override only what each `it` needs.

## Why

- Reduces noise and repetition in each test.
- Makes scenario-specific overrides easier to read.
- Keeps default fixture shape consistent across the file.

## Preferred pattern

```ts
let users: UserRepository;
let jwt: JwtAuthService;

beforeEach(() => {
  users = {
    create: vi.fn(),
    findById: vi.fn(),
    findByEmailWithPasswordHash: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(defaultUser),
    update: vi.fn(),
    delete: vi.fn(),
  };

  jwt = {
    signAccess: vi.fn().mockReturnValue("access-jwt"),
    signRefresh: vi.fn().mockReturnValue("refresh-jwt"),
    signForgotPasswordToken: vi.fn(),
    verifyForgotPasswordToken: vi.fn(),
    verifyAccess: vi.fn(),
    verifyRefresh: vi.fn(),
  };
});

it("fails when user is not authenticated", async () => {
  users.findByEmailWithPasswordHash = vi.fn().mockResolvedValue({
    ...defaultUser,
    authenticated: false,
  });
  // test assertions...
});
```

## Avoid

Do not redefine large dependency objects in every `it` with many unchanged or empty fields.

```ts
it("some case", async () => {
  const users = {
    create: vi.fn(),
    findById: vi.fn(),
    findByEmailWithPasswordHash: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(...),
    findMany: vi.fn(),
    update: vi.fn(),
    delete: vi.fn(),
    // repeated again in each test...
  };
  // ...
});
```
